{
  "mechanism": "Replace in-sample backward induction with leave-one-out ridge LSM. The parent uses each path both to fit its continuation value and to decide that path’s exercise action, creating influential-path bias with only 256 paths. Analytic leave-one-out predictions provide an approximately out-of-sample stopping decision without sacrificing data to fixed folds.",
  "implementation": "At each time step, construct and standardize the existing feature matrix. Fit ridge regression to the current downstream cash-flow targets, selecting lambda from a small fixed log-grid such as [1e-4, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1, 1, 10] by generalized cross-validation. From the selected ridge fit compute fitted values yhat, residuals r, and leverage h_i = x_i^T(X^T X + lambda I)^{-1}x_i. Use continuation_i_LOO = yhat_i - h_i*r_i/max(1-h_i, 1e-3) solely to update each training path’s exercise/cash-flow decision. Store the ordinary full-sample ridge coefficient, mean, and scale for prediction, preserving the parent model format and predict routine. Do not penalize the intercept.",
  "expected_behavior": "Exercise labels should become less sensitive to individual high-leverage paths and noisy continuation targets, yielding a more stable boundary and higher genuinely out-of-sample discounted payoff. Unlike fixed K-fold cross-fitting, every decision uses 255 training observations and the calculation requires only small linear solves, comfortably fitting the 30-second budget. The ridge references’ scores motivate retaining a linear representation, but do not establish that leave-one-out induction is causal; the proposed gain comes specifically from changing how training-path stopping decisions are generated.",
  "failure_condition": "This can fail if feature dimension is large enough that leverages approach one, if the GCV-selected penalty is unstable across backward steps, or if deliberate in-sample optimism happens to improve the tested policy. Guard against numerical explosions by clipping 1-h_i, rejecting non-finite candidates, and falling back to a moderate fixed ridge penalty."
}
